              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Peierls bracket
            (
            %a{:href => "#Peierls52"}> Peierls 52
            ) is equivalently the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
            such as of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            (
            %a{:href => "#DutschFredenhagen01"}> Dütsch-Fredenhagen 01
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Khavkine14"} Khavkine 14
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Collini16"} Collini 16, prop. 31 and below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            satisfied by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            fields to have unique
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retarded+and+advanced+Green+functions"} retarded and advanced Green functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            to the space of all field configurations (
            %a{:href => "#Marolf94"}> Marolf 94
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DuetschFredenhagen03"} Dütsch-Fredenhagen 03, section 2.1
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FredenhagenRejzner12"} Fredenhagen-Rejzner 12, section 5
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/off-shell+Poisson+bracket"} off-shell Poisson bracket
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls+bracket"} Peierls bracket
            of two suitably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configuration space is the antisymmetrized influence on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            perturbation of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge-fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            by a function that restricts to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on the embedding of the space of solutions in the field configuration space. It is the construction of the influence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            that requires the existence of unique retarded and advanced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green%27s+functions"} Green's functions
            of the linearized field equations. One can avoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            the action, as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            any representative of the gauge equivalence class of Green’s functions with appropriate causal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
          %p
            For example, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrodynamics"} electrodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+equations"} Maxwell equations
            in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F=\mathbf{d}A
            and correspond to unique gauge equivalence classes of Green’s functions for Maxwell equations in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+potential"} vector potential
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on the space of field configurations becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
            in the following way. Pick a set of functions on the space of field configurations that restrict to a non-degeneratce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+system"} coordinate system
            on the embedded
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            conditions define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bivector"} Poisson bivector
            by being declared canonical, such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
            of the bivector coincides with the ideal generated by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            conditions. Obviously the Poisson structure thus constructed on the algebra of functions on field configurations is not unique and depends on the above choice of coordinates; the same non-uniqueness may be parametrized instead by a choice of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            on the space of field configurations. The embedded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            becomes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leaf"} symplectic leaf
            of the symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            of the space of field configurations.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#the_peirls_bracket The Peirls bracket
          %blockquote
            %p under construction
          #PeirlsFormula.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p (…)
            %p
              …suitable PDE with advanced/retarded
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green%27s+function"} Green's function
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_S^{A/R}
              %em causal Gree’s function
              is their difference
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi S
                    %mo ≔
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi S
                      %mi R
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi S
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_S \coloneqq \Delta_S^{R} - \Delta_S^A
            %p (…)
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Khavkine14"}> #Khavkine 14, def. 3.9
            )
          %h3#the_offshell_peierls_bracket The off-shell Peierls bracket
          %p
            The idea of (
            %a{:href => "#Marolf93"}> Marolf 93
            , section II) is this:
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q(0), p(0)\}
            is the given (symplectic) Poisson bracket on the space of solutions, identified with the space of initial data, then requiring that everything Poisson-commutes with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi EL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange functional
            of the action, uniquely extends this to a bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q(t_1), p(t_2)\}
            on all hisories, because commutation with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S)
            involves generation of time translation. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S)
            generates a Poisson ideal and dividing that out reproduces the original symplectic bracket.
          %p
            Now observe (Khavkine 1) that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S)
            provides a foliation of history space by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leaves"} symplectic leaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi const
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S) \mapsto EL(S) - const
            the above still goes through. Observe also (Khavkine 2) that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi const
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(S) - const = 0
            is the equations of motion for the origial action with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi J
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J q
            added. Hence the off-shell Peierls Poisson structure has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+leaves"} symplectic leaves
            parameterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
          %p
            Observe finally (Khavkine 3) that with (
            %a{:href => "#Marolf93"}> Marolf 93, section III B
            ) it follows that the Peierls bracket on the shifted leaves agrees with the original one. (…)
          %h2#properties Properties
          #PeierlsPoissonBracket.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Peierls bracket is the canonical Poisson bracket in field theory)
            %p
              Given a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+field+theory"} local Lagrangian field theory
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetries"} gauge symmetries
              are globally recognizable (
              %a{:href => "#Khavkine14"}> Khavkine 14, section 3.2.2
              ). Then the Peierls bracket (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PeirlsFormula"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
              corresponding to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+space"} reduced
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Khavkine14"}> Khavkine 14, theorem 3.2
            )
          %h2#references References
          %p The definition of what now is called the Peierls bracket originates in
          %ul
            %li#Peierls52
              R. Peierls,
              %em The commutation laws of relativistic field theory
              (1952) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/pss/99080"}> jstor
              )
          %p
            In this article the Peierls bracket on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} non-gauge
            system is defined and the equivalence with the Hamiltonian phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+structure"} symplectic structure
            is (incompletely) demonstrated. Peierls also discusses how the definition extends to gauge theories and to fermionic theories.
          %p An early review is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+DeWitt"}> Bryce DeWitt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Spacetime Approach to Quantum Field Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+DeWitt"} Bryce DeWitt
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Stora"} Raymond Stora
              (eds.),
              %em Les Houches Session XL, Relativity, Groups and Topology II
              (North-Holland, 1983), pp. 382–738.
          %p
            which is also the first to explicitly check the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            for the Peirls bracket.
          %p A streamlined general account is in
          %ul
            %li#Khavkine14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"}> Igor Khavkine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Covariant phase space, constraints, gauge and the Peierls formula
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1282"} arXiv.1402.1282
          %p A traditional physics textbook account amplifying the Peierls bracket is
          %ul
            %li#DeWitt03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bryce+DeWitt"}> Bryce DeWitt
              ,
              %em The global approach to Quantum Field Theory
              (2 volumes), Oxford 2003
          %p
            The fact that the Peirls bracket for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            gives the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#DutschFredenhagen01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative algebraic field theory, and deformation quantization
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Longo"} Roberto Longo
                (ed.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematical Physics in Mathematics and Physics, Quantum and Operator Algebraic Aspects
              %p
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0101079"}> arXiv:hep-th/0101079
                )
            %li#Collini16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giovanni+Collini"}> Giovanni Collini
                , prop. 31 and below in
                %em Fedosov Quantization and Perturbative Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.09626"}> arXiv:1603.09626
                )
          %p
            The off-shell generalization to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on configuration space (history space) was first given in
          %ul
            %li#Marolf93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Don+Marolf"}> Don Marolf
                ,
                %em Poisson Brackets on the Space of Histories
                Annals of Physics Volume 236, Issue 2, December 1994, Pages 374-391 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308141"}> arXiv:hep-th/9308141
                )
            %li#Marolf94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Don+Marolf"}> Don Marolf
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em The Generalized Peierls Bracket
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308150"} arXiv:hep-th/9308150
          %p See also exercise 17.12 in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantization+of+Gauge+Systems"} Quantization of Gauge Systems
          %p
            A mathematically clean account of the (on- and off-shell) Peierls bracket (for
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Master Ward Identity and Generalized Schwinger-Dyson Equation in Classical Field Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0211242"} arXiv:hep-th/0211242
          %p and in section 2 of
          %ul
            %li
              Ferdinand Brennecke,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
              = succeed "," do
                %em Removal of violations of the Master Ward Identity in perturbative QFT
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv-web3.library.cornell.edu/abs/0705.3160"} arXiv:0705.3160
          %p
            there with an eye towards the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            program of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
            (pAQFT) on flat and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} on curved spacetimes
          %ul
            %li#DuetschFredenhagen04
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Causal perturbation theory in terms of retarded products, and a proof of the action Ward identity
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0403213"} arXiv:hep-th/0403213
          %p Further references to its use in the renormalization program of pAQFT can be found in:
          %ul
            %li#FredenhagenRejzener12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
              = succeed "." do
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in the functional approach to classical field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5112"} arXiv:1101.5112
          %p Functional analytic aspects of the definition and existence of the Peierls bracket (including its off-shell extension) are discussed in section 3.2 of
          %ul
            %li#BrunettiFredenhagenRibeiro12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pedro+Ribeiro"} Pedro Ribeiro
              %em Algebraic Structure of Classical Field Theory I: Kinematics and Linearized Dynamics for Real Scalar Fields
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1209.2148"}> arXiv:1209.2148
              )
          %p
            The equivalence between the Peierls bracket and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of classical field theory (by showing the equivalence of both to the canonical Poisson bracket in Hamiltonian formalism) was demonstrated in
          %ul
            %li#BarnichHenneauxSchomblond91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"} Marc Henneaux
              = succeed "." do
                %em Covariant description of the canonical formalism
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/physrevd.44.r939"} doi
          %p
            Further discussion of the manifestly covariant equivalence between the Peierls bracket and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of classical field theory (avoiding traditional proof via the canonical Hamiltonian formalism) can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Forger"}> Michael Forger
                , Sandro Romero,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Covariant Poisson Brackets in Geometric Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0408008"} arXiv:math-ph/0408008
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"}> Igor Khavkine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Characteristics, Conal Geometry and Causality in Locally Covariant Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.1914"} arXiv:1211.1914
        .revisedby
